    <body>
      <div type="clause" n="20.1">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:IICn">II Cn</ref> 20.1</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="20.1" corresp="#wit-g-a"><pb n="324" ed="#liebermann"/>swa for frigne swa for þeowne; ac we nellað geðafian þ<hi rend="italic">æt</hi> unriht.<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.1-3"/> Manig strec man wyle, gyf<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="15" target="#IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.15"/> he mæg ⁊ mot, werian his man, swa hwæðer swa him þingð<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="21" target="#IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.21"/>, þ<hi rend="italic">æt</hi> he hine eað<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="22" target="#IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.22"/> awerian mæge,</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="20.1" corresp="#wit-b"><pb n="324" ed="#liebermann"/>frigne swá for ðeowne; ac we nyllað geðafian þ<hi rend="italic">æt</hi> unriht. Manig strǽc man wyle, gyf he mæg ⁊ mot, werian his man, swa hwæðer swa hi<hi rend="italic">m</hi> ðincð, þ<hi rend="italic">æt</hi> he hine yð awerian mǽge, swá for</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="20.1" corresp="#wit-a"><pb n="324" ed="#liebermann"/>for ðeowne<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.1-4"/>; ac we nellað geþafian þ<hi rend="italic">æt</hi> unriht. Manig strec man wyle, gif he mæg ⁊ mot, werian his man, swa hwæðer<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="4" target="#IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.4"/> him þincð, þ<hi rend="italic">æt</hi> he hine awerian mæge, swa for frigne swa</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="20.1" corresp="#wit-q"><pb n="323" ed="#liebermann"/>Multi strecman<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="9" target="#IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.9"/> (id est potentes siue fortes) uolunt, si possint et audeant, defendere homines suos ad utrumlibet, sicut eis<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="10" target="#IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.10-2"/> uidebitur, quod tunc procedat, modo pro seruo modo pro libero; sed nolumus hanc iniusticiam<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.1-2"/> pati.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="20.1" corresp="#wit-inst-cn"><pb n="323" ed="#liebermann"/>Multi fortes homines uolunt cum<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="23" target="#IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.23"/> ingenio defendere in placitis suos subiectos<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="24" target="#IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.24"/>, quo leuius illis posse uidetur, pro seruis aut pro liberis; sed nos<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.1-5"/> huic iniusticie<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.2"/> consentire nolumus.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="20.1" corresp="#wit-cons-cn"><pb n="323" ed="#liebermann"/>Multus austerus homo uult (omne), si potest et sibi licet, tueri suum hominem utro modo, siue pro libero siue pro seruo, quo facilius eum tueri poterit; sed nolumus illam iniuriam pati.</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="ang" source="#robertson" n="20.1" corresp="#wit-b"><pb n="184" ed="#robertson"/>Manig strǽc man wyle, gyf he mæg ⁊ mot, werian his man, swa hwæðer swa him ðincð þæt he hine y<ptr type="editorNote" n="8" target="#IICn_20-1.robnote.fn.8"/>ð awerian mǽge—swá for frigne swá for ðeowne—ac we nyllað geðafian þæt unriht.</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="ang" source="#rabin-doml" corresp="#wit-g-a"><pb n="264" ed="#rabin-doml"/>Manig strec man wyle, gyf he mæg and mot, werian his man, swa hwæðer swa him þingð, þæt he hine eað awerian mæge, swa for frigne swa for þeowne; ac we nellað geðafian þæt unriht.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="de" source="#liebermann"><pb n="323" ed="#liebermann"/>Mancher<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#IICn_20-1.lnote.de.1"/> gewaltige<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#IICn_20-1.lnote.de.2"/> Mann will, wenn er kann und darf, seinen untergebenen Mann, je wie ihm dünkt, dass er ihn bequem vertheidigen<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="3" target="#IICn_20-1.lnote.de.3"/> könne, als eines von beiden<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="4" target="#IICn_20-1.lnote.de.4"/> gerichtlich vertreten, [nämlich] bald als freien, bald als unfreien; aber wir wollen dies Unrecht nicht dulden.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude" ana="#fromGerman">Many a powerful man wishes, if he can and may, to defend his man in court as whichever of the two seems to him easier to defend, either as a free man or as a slave; but we will not allow this injustice.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#robertson" corresp="#wit-b"><pb n="185" ed="#robertson"/>Many self-assertive<ptr type="editorNote" n="1" target="#IICn_20-1.robnote.n.1"/> men seek, if they can and may, to protect their men<ptr type="editorNote" n="2" target="#IICn_20-1.robnote.n.2"/> in whichever way it seems to them that they can do so the more easily—namely, by representing them either as freemen or slaves—but we will not permit this injustice.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#whitelock"><pb n="464" ed="#whitelock"/>Many an overbearing man will, if he can and may, defend his man whichever way it seems to him that he can defend him more easily, whether as a free man or a slave; but we will not allow that abuse.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#rabin-doml" corresp="#wit-g-a"><pb n="265" ed="#rabin-doml"/>Many recalcitrant individuals, if they may and can, seek to maintain control over their underlings by whatever means that they think easiest, either by treating them as if they were free or as if they were slaves; but we will not allow this injustice.</ab>
        <note type="editorial">BR: added from Gesetze I p. 323, October 2026 (MS Cons); I: first page of the clause in Liebermann, checked against Liebermann, Gesetze I, October 2026 (e.g. was “322”, now “323”; 1 rows of II Cn)</note>
        <note type="textual" source="#rabin-doml">eað: omitted A</note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.10" n="10" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">323</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">multum <hi rend="italic">Hk</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">multum Hk</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.5" n="5" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">323</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">fehlt Hr</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">lacking in Hr</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.11" n="11" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">323</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">tu. h. s. tueri ultro m., pro <hi rend="italic">Hk</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">tu. h. s. tueri ultro m., pro Hk</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.1" n="1" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">323</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">fehlt Hk</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">lacking in Hk</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.9" n="9" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">323</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">sterc. <hi rend="italic">KHk</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">sterc. KHk</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.10-2" n="10" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">323</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">u. eis <hi rend="italic">K;</hi> factu facilius sit <hi rend="italic">Hn.</hi> 78, 2 <hi rend="italic">genauer</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">u. eis K; factu facilius sit Hn. 78, 2, more accurately</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.1-2" n="1" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">325</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">iust. <hi rend="italic">K</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">iust. K</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.1-3" n="1" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">324</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">-hte <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">-hte Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.15" n="15" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">322</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">gif <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">gif Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.21" n="21" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">322</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">ðincþ <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">ðincþ Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.22" n="22" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">322</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">fehlt Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">lacking in Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.1-4" n="1" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">324</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">de. <hi rend="italic">A</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">de. A</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.4" n="4" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">322</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">hwæder <hi rend="italic">A</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">hwæder A</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.23" n="23" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">323</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">d. c. ing. suos in p., quo <hi rend="italic">Lb</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">d. c. ing. suos in p., quo Lb</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.24" n="24" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">323</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">fehlt Lb S</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">lacking in Lb S</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.1-5" n="1" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">325</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">h. i. nos <hi rend="italic">H</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">h. i. nos H</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="IICn_20-1.lnote.oe.2" n="2" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">325</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">iust. <hi rend="italic">Lb</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">iust. Lb</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_20-1.lnote.de.1" n="1" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">205</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Bessere Übs. ‘gar ma.’ = viele.—Aus Q und Orig. benutzt durch Hn 78, 2</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Better translation ‘gar ma.’ = many.—Used from Q and the original by Hn 78, 2</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_20-1.lnote.de.2" n="2" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">205</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">‘mächtig’ Schmid, doch auch ‘gewaltsam’. <hi rend="italic">Vgl. Gl</hi> unfrei 3c; frei 8. a. Durch Eintritt in Hundred und Zehnerschaft sollte der Status sich klar erweisen; darum wohl steht der Satz hier; Waitz <hi rend="italic">Dt. Verf. G</hi>. I 446</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">‘mighty’ Schmid, but also ‘violent’. <hi rend="italic">Cf. Gl</hi> unfrei 3c; frei 8. a. Entry into hundred and tithing was meant to establish a man's status clearly; that is probably why the sentence stands here; Waitz <hi rend="italic">Dt. Verf. G</hi>. I 446</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_20-1.lnote.de.3" n="3" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">205</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">bessere Übs.: ‘bequemer herausreissen, sichernd schützen’: <hi rend="italic">awerian</hi> bed. den Erfolg des <hi rend="italic">werian</hi> 2 Zeilen vorher</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">better translation: ‘more easily snatch away, protect securely’: <hi rend="italic">awerian</hi> means the success of the <hi rend="italic">werian</hi> 2 lines before</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="IICn_20-1.lnote.de.4" n="4" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">205</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Zur Unsicherheit des Status <hi rend="italic">vgl. Gl</hi> Bauer 9 und Urk.: <hi rend="italic">Wurcon filii et nepotes posteritasque omnis defenderunt se per iuramentum Eadgari regis permissu, quoniam accusatione malorum dicebantur patres eorum fuisse coloni regis</hi>; Thorpe <hi rend="italic">Dipl</hi>. 631</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">On the uncertainty of status <hi rend="italic">cf. Gl</hi> Bauer 9 and the charter: <hi rend="italic">Wurcon filii et nepotes posteritasque omnis defenderunt se per iuramentum Eadgari regis permissu, quoniam accusatione malorum dicebantur patres eorum fuisse coloni regis</hi>; Thorpe <hi rend="italic">Dipl</hi>. 631</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="IICn_20-1.robnote.n.1" n="1" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">355</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">strǽc</hi> is rendered <hi rend="italic">potentes sive fortes</hi> in Q; <hi rend="italic">fortes</hi> in Inst.; <hi rend="italic">austerus</hi> in Cons. Schmid has ‘mächtig,’ Liebermann ‘gewaltig.’</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="IICn_20-1.robnote.n.2" n="2" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">355</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">Lit. ‘a man of his’ or ‘one of his men.’</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="IICn_20-1.robnote.fn.8" n="8" source="#robertson"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">184</biblScope></bibl><label>yð</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">Om. A, Ld.</seg></note>
      </div>
